sed -e '/regex/!b
         i\
BEFORE
         a\
AFTER' inputfile

or,

sed -e '/regex/!b
         s/^/BEFORE\
/;s/$/\
AFTER/' inputfile

(note: there should be nothing after the trailing \ above, as they are
there to escape the newline)

using nawk:

nawk '/regex/{$0="BEFORE\n"$0"\nAFTER"}1' inputfile

or with perl:

perl -ple '$_="BEFORE\n$_\nAFTER"if/regex/' inputfile

